 ## Overview
-Russian Roulette is a probabilistic technique used to terminate volumetric paths or ray marching loops when their contribution to the final image becomes negligible, thereby reducing computational cost while maintaining statistical unbiasedness [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike deterministic early termination which clamps the result and introduces bias, Russian Roulette scales the weight of surviving paths to compensate for the probability of termination, ensuring the expected value remains unchanged [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinct from [Empty-space skipping](1002-empty-space-skipping.md) (which avoids sampling empty regions) and [Early ray termination](1019-early-ray-termination.md) (which stops based on opacity thresholds without compensation) by explicitly handling the variance and bias trade-off through random sampling [S2, unofficial].
+Russian Roulette is a probabilistic technique used to terminate volumetric paths or ray marching loops when their contribution to the final image becomes negligible, thereby reducing computational cost while maintaining statistical unbiasedness [S1, S2, unofficial][S3]. Unlike deterministic early termination which clamps the result and introduces bias, Russian Roulette scales the weight of surviving paths to compensate for the probability of termination, ensuring the expected value remains unchanged [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is applied during the ray marching or path integration loop after opacity or transmittance updates [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-- Input: Current path throughput/weight $W$ (or transmittance $T$), random number generator.
-- Output: Updated weight $W'$ (or $T'$) and a boolean indicating whether the path continues or terminates.
